1339. Maximum Product of Splitted Binary Tree — Explanation (for README.md)
Problem
You are given the root of a binary tree. You can split the tree into two subtrees by removing exactly one edge.
For that split, compute:
	•	sumA = sum of all node values in the first subtree
	•	sumB = sum of all node values in the second subtree

Your goal is to maximize sumA * sumB.
Because the product can be huge, return the result modulo (10^9 + 7).
Important: maximize the product first, then apply modulo.

Key Observation
If we remove the edge above some subtree whose node-sum is s, then:
	•	One part has sum s
	•	The other part has sum totalSum - s
So the product for that cut is:
product = s * (totalSum - s)
That means we just need:
	1.	The total sum of the entire tree
	2.	Every subtree sum s, because every subtree corresponds to one possible cut
Then we take the maximum of s * (totalSum - s) over all subtrees.

Approach
Two-phase idea (can be done in one traversal + loop):
	1.	Compute all subtree sums (postorder traversal).
	2.	Let totalSum be the subtree sum of the root.
	3.	For each subtree sum s, compute s * (totalSum - s) and keep the maximum.
	4.	Return maxProduct % (10^9 + 7).
To avoid recursion depth issues (tree can have up to 50,000 nodes), we use an iterative postorder traversal.

Why this works
	•	Every possible split is done by removing one edge.
	•	Removing an edge always separates the tree into:
	•	a subtree (sum s)
	•	the rest of the tree (sum total - s)
	•	Checking all subtree sums guarantees we consider every possible cut.
	•	Taking the max gives the best possible product.

Complexity
	•	Time: O(n) (each node processed once)
	•	Space: O(n) (store subtree sums)
